# ---------------- Introduction
T("【Introduction.】 ⟦Machine-learning models are increasingly trained on features engineered from high-throughput sequencing data. In such data the number of features exceeds the number of independent samples by several orders of magnitude, and the feature-engineering pipeline, rather than the classifier, often decides what a model can learn (Whalen et al., 2022). With few independent samples, any dependence between training and test data and any data-dependent step fitted outside the training folds inflates the estimated accuracy (Vabalas et al., 2019; Kapoor and Narayanan, 2023). Public single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) cohorts are a typical case: they contain a handful of biological samples, each sequenced in several runs, and their reads pass through a long chain of alignment, variant calling and filtering before a feature matrix exists.⟧")
T("Besides gene expression, scRNA-seq reads contain expressed single-nucleotide variants and short insertions and deletions. Turning these calls into gene-level features is attractive for machine learning, but every step of the chain – alignment, calling, filtering, recurrence selection and aggregation – changes the resulting features, and small public cohorts with several sequencing runs per sample make the evaluation of such features fragile. A previous analysis of the cohort used here showed that a gene-burden classifier reached a balanced accuracy of 0.973 under run-level cross-validation but only 0.708 under sample-grouped validation, without significance in a sample-level permutation test (Kunikeyev et al., 2026). Before new models are built on such features, the pipeline that produces them needs to be described and audited in its own right.", hl=True)
T("The aim of this work is therefore an engineering one: to describe a reproducible pipeline that converts scRNA-seq alignments into expressed-variant gene-burden features and to characterise its behaviour. The contributions are: (1) a fully specified GATK-based processing chain with explicit acceptance rules, recurrence selection and gene aggregation, including a training-only variant for validation; (2) a reconciliation of condition labels with the public source; (3) an audit of the outputs – call counts and filters, coverage dependence, sensitivity of the recurrence threshold and computational cost; and (4) a grouped validation check against an exact permutation null, together with a checklist for similar studies. The remainder of the paper reviews related work, details the pipeline and the audit, reports the results and discusses their implications.", hl=True)

# ---------------- Literature review (72-74 kept)
T("【Literature review.】 Most scRNA-seq studies emphasize gene expression. However, aligned reads also contain expressed single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) and short insertions/deletions (INDELs) (Liu et al., 2019). Aggregating such observations by gene can reduce the dimensionality of sparse site-level calls and yield a gene-burden representation ⟦suitable for standard classifiers⟧. It is also technically difficult: an RNA-derived call is conditional on expression and read coverage and may reflect mapping ambiguity, RNA editing, or allelic imbalance rather than a confirmed genomic mutation (Dou et al., 2024).", hl=False)
b.keep(73); b.keep(74)
T("Machine learning can integrate many weak gene-level signals, but limited cohorts create a high risk of optimistic performance (Vabalas et al., 2019). ⟦Cross-validated estimates become optimistic whenever a data-dependent step, such as feature selection, is fitted outside the training folds (Varma and Simon, 2006), and reviews of machine learning in genomics list dependence between training and test observations – for example, several runs of one donor – among the most common sources of leakage (Whalen et al., 2022). A survey of published studies found leakage in many scientific fields and proposed reporting standards to detect it (Kapoor and Narayanan, 2023). For expressed-variant features both mechanisms are present at once: runs of one sample share most of their germline alleles, and recurrent loci are usually selected on the whole cohort.⟧", hl=False)
T("Reproducibility of the processing chain is a separate requirement. Variant calling from RNA follows established best practices – splitting reads at splice junctions, haplotype-based calling and hard filtering when no truth set is available (McKenna et al., 2010; Van der Auwera and O'Connor, 2020) – but benchmarks show that the results depend strongly on depth, caller and filtering design (Liu et al., 2019; Wiens et al., 2024). Calls from scRNA-seq can even be used to verify cell-line identity (Ramazzotti et al., 2022), which shows how strongly they encode the individual. To our knowledge, no study has reported, for one scRNA-seq cohort, the complete path from reads to gene-burden features together with filter statistics, coverage dependence, threshold sensitivity, runtime and a grouped validation check; this gap is addressed here.", hl=True)

# ---------------- Materials and methods
T("【Materials and methods.】 ⟪Data and labels.⟫ ⟦The source data were obtained from the public BioProject PRJNA736095 (GEO series GSE176415), a public scRNA-seq series of human wound-edge and unwounded-skin samples (Singh et al., 2022). The single-cell series contains seven GEO samples, each sequenced as two SRA runs: four unwounded-skin samples (Sc_Skin1–Sc_Skin4, eight runs) and three wound-edge samples (Sc_W1–Sc_W3, six runs). Condition labels were taken directly from the GEO sample titles (Table 1). The labels of the previous version of this manuscript, described as seven wound-exposed and seven unexposed samples, were inverted relative to GEO for all 14 runs and did not match the 4/3 sample structure; they are not used in this revision, and all results reported here were recomputed from the raw calls. GEO does not provide donor identifiers, so each sample was initially treated as a separate donor; this assumption was tested by genotype concordance (see below).⟧", hl=False)
