T("⟪Alignment and variant calling.⟫ A STAR genome index was built from the GRCh38 primary assembly with GENCODE release 43 annotation and a splice-junction overhang of 100 bases. STARsolo was run in CB_UMI_Simple mode with a 16-base cell barcode and a 12-base unique molecular identifier, without a barcode whitelist, and produced coordinate-sorted BAM files carrying cell-barcode and UMI tags; the alignments were indexed with SAMtools (Dobin et al., 2013; Danecek et al., 2021). ⟦Variants were then called separately for each run with GATK 4.6.2.0 (McKenna et al., 2010; Van der Auwera and O'Connor, 2020): AddOrReplaceReadGroups, SplitNCigarReads, HaplotypeCaller with soft-clipped bases ignored and a calling-confidence threshold of 20, VariantFiltration and SelectVariants. A record was accepted when it passed all four hard filters in (1):⟧", hl=False)
b.equation(81, sub(r("A"), r("sv")) + r("=1") + d(r("QD≥2, FS≤30, MQ≥40, SOR≤3"), "[", "]"), 1)
T("⟦All accepted records are described as expressed variant records, not as validated germline or somatic mutations, because RNA-derived calls are conditional on expression and coverage and may include RNA editing and mapping artefacts (Liu et al., 2019; Dou et al., 2024; Muyas et al., 2024).⟧", hl=False)
T("⟪Recurrent loci and gene burden.⟫ ⟦Let V_{s} be the accepted loci of run s and S the set of runs used to build features. The support f_{v} of locus v is the number of runs in S that carry it, and the recurrent set R contains loci with support of at least k runs and a maximum variant allele fraction of at least 0.05, as in (2). The pipeline default is k = 4. Recurrent loci were intersected with GENCODE exons (Frankish et al., 2019), and the burden of gene g in run s counts the recurrent loci of s that overlap g, as in (3), where M_{vg} = 1 if locus v overlaps an exon of g.⟧", hl=False)
b.equation(83, sub(r("f"), r("v")) + r("=") + nary("∑", r("s∈S"), "", r("1") + d(r("v∈") + sub(r("V"), r("s")), "[", "]")) + r(",   R=") + d(r("v: ") + sub(r("f"), r("v")) + r("≥k,  max ") + sub(r("VAF"), r("sv")) + r("≥0.05"), "{", "}"), 2)
b.equation(84, sub(r("GB"), r("s,g")) + r("=") + nary("∑", r("v∈R∩") + sub(r("V"), r("s")), "", sub(r("M"), r("vg"))), 3)
T("⟦In the global mode S contains all 14 runs. In the training-only mode, used for validation, R and the burden matrix are rebuilt inside every fold from the training runs only, so held-out runs cannot influence which loci become features. Expression features were pseudobulk counts per run transformed to log1p(CPM) for 18,860 genes.⟧", hl=False)
T("【⟪Audit of the pipeline.⟫】", hl=True)
T(f"⟦Five audit modules were applied to the outputs. (a) Call counts: raw, PASS and recurrent records per run and the frequency of each hard-filter flag. (b) Coverage dependence: Spearman correlation of PASS calls with the median read depth at PASS calls, the number of detected genes in the expression matrix and the BAM size, at run level and for sample means; the condition difference in calls was tested exactly over all 35 ways of labelling three of the seven samples as wound edge. (c) Threshold sensitivity: the recurrence threshold k was varied from 1 to 14, and for each value the numbers of loci and genes, the share of SNVs and the Jaccard similarity J of the recurrent loci of every run pair were recorded, as in (4). Pairs were grouped into runs of the same sample, of different samples with the same condition and of different samples with different conditions; for each k the share of runs whose most similar run came from the same sample (1-NN identification) and the exact p-value of the condition effect were computed. (d) Computational cost: wall-clock time of each GATK step from the run logs, Java memory and storage of the intermediate files. (e) Identity: genotype concordance (heterozygous or homozygous alternative) on calls with read depth of at least 20 in both runs.⟧", hl=False)
b.equation(85, r("J") + d(r("s,t")) + r("=") + f(d(sub(r("V"), r("s")) + r("∩") + sub(r("V"), r("t")), "|", "|"), d(sub(r("V"), r("s")) + r("∪") + sub(r("V"), r("t")), "|", "|")), 4)
T("【⟪Grouped validation check.⟫】", hl=True)
T("⟦To test whether the features carry condition information, six classifiers with fixed hyperparameters were evaluated: L2-regularised logistic regression, a multilayer perceptron, a 500-tree random forest, an RBF-kernel support vector machine, k-nearest neighbours and XGBoost (Pedregosa et al., 2011; Breiman, 2001; Cortes and Vapnik, 1995; Chen and Guestrin, 2016). Variance filtering and standardisation were fitted inside each training fold. Validation left out one GEO sample at a time (seven folds) or one individual at a time (six folds, see Results); held-out run probabilities were averaged per sample, and the balanced accuracy (5) was computed on the seven sample-level predictions. Because feature construction is label-free, the folds were built once and every model was refitted under all 35 labelings of the seven samples; the exact p-value is the share of labelings that reach at least the observed balanced accuracy, and its smallest attainable value is 1/35 ≈ 0.029.⟧", hl=False)
b.equation(97, r("BA=") + f(r("1"), r("2")) + d(f(r("TP"), r("TP+FN")) + r("+") + f(r("TN"), r("TN+FP"))), 5)
T("【⟪Coverage-normalised gene burden (new pipeline element).⟫】", hl=True)
T("⟦The previous analysis of this cohort (Kunikeyev et al., 2026) used raw burden counts. Because the number of calls depends on sequencing depth, a normalised burden was added to the pipeline in this work: the burden of each gene was divided by the number N_{s} of PASS calls of the run and multiplied by 10^{5}, as in (6). The normalisation uses only the run itself, so it does not use labels and cannot carry information between folds. Its effect was measured in three ways: the Spearman correlation of the total burden and of the burden of each gene with the median read depth; 1-NN sample identification on standardised features; and the L2-regularised logistic regression of the previous analysis, refitted on raw and on normalised burden under the three grouped designs, each with the exact test over 35 labelings.⟧")
b.equation(85, subsup(r("GB"), r("s,g"), r("norm")) + r("=") + f(sub(r("GB"), r("s,g")), sub(r("N"), r("s"))) + r("·") + sup(r("10"), r("5")), 6)
T("⟦All steps ran in Python 3 with scikit-learn 1.9.0 and XGBoost 3.2.0. Input files are identified by SHA-256 hashes in the result manifests, and per-fold feature counts and per-sample predictions are stored for every model and labeling.⟧", hl=False)
b.picture(107, str(FIG / "Figure_1_pipeline.png"), FIGW)
CAP("Figure 1 – Reproducible pipeline from scRNA-seq reads to gene-burden features and its audit outputs")

# ---------------- Results
T(f"【Results.】 ⟪Call counts and filters.⟫ ⟦HaplotypeCaller produced {fint(qc.raw.min())}–{fint(qc.raw.max())} raw records per run. The hard filters flagged {qc.pct_removed_by_hard_filters.min():.1f}–{qc.pct_removed_by_hard_filters.max():.1f}% of them (median 18.3%); the most frequent flag was strand-odds ratio (SOR > 3), followed by low quality-by-depth (QD < 2) and Fisher strand bias (FS > 30), and a record could carry several flags (Figure 2). Per run, {fint(per.n_pass.min())}–{fint(per.n_pass.max())} PASS records remained (median 1,272,246.5; 16,283,367 in total), of which {100*per.n_snp.sum()/per.n_pass.sum():.1f}% were SNVs; 23.6–35.6% were homozygous for the alternative allele. Coverage at accepted calls was low: the median read depth per run was 5–7, and only 27.2–40.1% of calls had depth of at least 10. The union of all runs contained 9,528,556 distinct loci. The recurrence rule with k = 4 retained 882,138 loci in the pipeline output (883,831 in the independent re-implementation used for the audit, a difference of 0.19% caused by multi-allelic records), mapped to 27,230 genes through 944,972 locus–gene pairs; each run kept {qc.pct_pass_retained_as_recurrent.min():.0f}–{qc.pct_pass_retained_as_recurrent.max():.0f}% of its PASS calls as recurrent.⟧", hl=False)
b.picture(107, str(FIG / "Figure_2_call_counts_qc.png"), FIGW)
CAP("Figure 2 – Raw, PASS and recurrent records per run (A) and share of raw records flagged by each hard filter (B); the dotted line separates unwounded-skin and wound-edge runs")
T(f"⟪Coverage dependence.⟫ ⟦The number of PASS calls followed sequencing and expression depth rather than tissue state (Figure 3). Across runs it correlated with the median depth at PASS calls (ρ = {c('median_dp').spearman_rho:.2f}, p = {c('median_dp').p:.1g}), with the number of detected genes (ρ = {c('detected_genes').spearman_rho:.2f}, p = {c('detected_genes').p:.1g}) and, more weakly, with BAM size (ρ = {c('bam_gb').spearman_rho:.2f}, p = {c('bam_gb').p:.2f}; 13 runs). The correlation with detected genes persisted for sample means (ρ = {c('detected_genes','GSM').spearman_rho:.2f}, p = {c('detected_genes','GSM').p:.3f}; seven samples). Wound-edge samples had on average 362,344 more PASS calls than unwounded samples, but this difference was not significant (exact p = 0.171), and it was also not significant after normalising calls by BAM size (p = 0.086). Raw call counts, and every feature that counts calls, therefore carry a strong technical component.⟧", hl=False)
b.picture(107, str(FIG / "Figure_3_coverage_dependence.png"), FIGW)
CAP("Figure 3 – PASS calls per run against median read depth, detected genes and BAM size")
T(f"⟪Sensitivity of the recurrence threshold.⟫ ⟦Figure 4 and Table 2 show how the threshold k shapes the feature space. Requiring support in two runs removed two-thirds of the 9.53 million loci; k = 4 left 883,831 loci, k = 7 left 218,634 and k = 14 only 2,677. The share of SNVs rose from 87.9% to 95.5% as k increased, because recurrent indels are rarer. Similarity between runs of the same sample stayed higher than between runs of different samples up to k = 12, and for every k from 1 to 10 the most similar run of each run was its partner from the same sample (1-NN identification 14/14). At the same time, pairs of different samples with the same condition were never more similar than pairs with different conditions: the exact p-value of the condition effect was {sw.p_condition_exact.min():.3f}–{sw.p_condition_exact.max():.3f} across all thresholds. Only at k ≥ 13, when the loci shared by nearly all runs remain, did sample identification disappear, together with almost all information. No threshold therefore converts the recurrent loci into condition-specific features; the choice of k mainly trades feature-space size against sample-specific noise.⟧", hl=False)
TCAP("Table 2 – Effect of the recurrence threshold k on the feature space and on run similarity")
rows = [["k (min. runs)", "Recurrent loci", "Genes", "SNV share, %", "Jaccard, same sample", "Jaccard, different samples", "1-NN sample identification", "Condition effect, exact p"]]
for kk in [1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14]:
    x = k.loc[kk]
    rows.append([str(kk), fint(x.n_loci), fint(x.n_genes) if kk >= 4 else "–", f"{x.pct_snp:.1f}", f"{x.within:.3f}", f"{x.between_all:.3f}", f"{int(round(14*x.nn_donor_id_acc))}/14", f"{x.p_condition_exact:.3f}"])
b.data_table(rows, [1.5, 2.0, 1.5, 1.6, 2.0, 2.2, 2.2, 2.0], font_size=18,
             note="Note: compiled by the authors from B_cohort_filter_sweep.tsv (Supplementary Table S3). Recurrent loci also require a maximum VAF ≥ 0.05. Gene counts are shown for k ≥ 4 because the variant-to-gene table covers recurrent loci only.")
b.picture(107, str(FIG / "Figure_4_filter_sensitivity.png"), FIGW)
CAP("Figure 4 – Recurrence-threshold sweep: size of the feature space (A), mean Jaccard similarity by pair class (B), and sample identification versus condition effect (C); the dashed line marks the default k = 4")
cp = comp
T(f"⟪Computational cost.⟫ ⟦GATK calling of the 14 runs took {total_h:.0f} wall-clock hours in total (Table 3, Figure 5). Two steps accounted for 96% of this time: SplitNCigarReads (median {cp.loc['SplitNCigarReads'].median_min/60:.1f} h per run, range {cp.loc['SplitNCigarReads'].min_min/60:.1f}–{cp.loc['SplitNCigarReads'].max_min/60:.1f} h) and HaplotypeCaller (median {cp.loc['HaplotypeCaller'].median_min/60:.1f} h, range {cp.loc['HaplotypeCaller'].min_min/60:.1f}–{cp.loc['HaplotypeCaller'].max_min/60:.1f} h), whereas filtering and selection took less than one minute per run. HaplotypeCaller time increased with BAM size (ρ = 0.64, p = 0.019). Each GATK job ran with a 4 GB Java heap and used 0.85–1.35 GB of it. The coordinate-sorted BAM files occupied 245 GB for the 13 runs whose BAM was archived, the 14 PASS VCF files 3.4 GB and the cohort VCF 0.18 GB; downstream steps – recurrence selection, gene mapping, gene burden and the complete validation with 35 labelings – ran in minutes on a workstation. Runs were processed in parallel, three with automatic resumption after interruption, so the elapsed calendar time was about five days.⟧", hl=False)
TCAP("Table 3 – Wall-clock time of the GATK steps per run")
rows = [["Step", "Runs logged", "Median, min", "Range, min", "Total, h"]]
for st in ["AddOrReplaceReadGroups", "SplitNCigarReads", "HaplotypeCaller", "VariantFiltration", "SelectVariants"]:
    x = cp.loc[st]
    rows.append([st, str(int(x.n_runs_logged)), f"{x.median_min:.1f}", f"{x.min_min:.1f}–{x.max_min:.1f}", f"{x.total_hours:.1f}"])
b.data_table(rows, [4.5, 2.2, 2.4, 3.0, 2.2], font_size=18,
             note="Note: compiled by the authors from the GATK completion audit (gatk_completion_audit.tsv; Supplementary Table S4). Read-group assignment was not logged for two runs (SRR14762240 and SRR14762241).")
b.picture(107, str(FIG / "Figure_5_runtime.png"), FIGW)
CAP("Figure 5 – Wall-clock time of the GATK steps for each run")
T(f"⟪Identity check and grouped validation.⟫ ⟦On calls with depth of at least 20, the two runs of each sample agreed in genotype class at 0.997–0.999 of shared loci, and runs of different samples at 0.936–0.973 – with one exception: runs of Sc_Skin1 (unwounded skin) and Sc_W2 (wound edge) agreed at 0.997, so these two samples come from one individual, and the seven samples represent six persons. Validation was therefore run both with sample-level and with person-level folds. With recurrent loci selected from the training runs only (636,538–797,513 loci per fold), all six classifiers assigned every held-out sample to the unwounded class and reached a balanced accuracy of exactly 0.500 under both groupings (exact p = {min(tr.p_ba.min(), pe.p_ba.min()):.2f}–{max(tr.p_ba.max(), pe.p_ba.max()):.2f}). With the global recurrent set, logistic regression reached 0.708, the value reported previously (Kunikeyev et al., 2026), with exact p = {gl.loc['Logistic regression (L2)'].p_ba:.3f}. The mean balanced accuracy over the 35 labelings was {tr.null_ba.min():.2f}–{tr.null_ba.max():.2f} with training-only loci, i.e. below 0.5, so 0.5 is not the chance level of these designs. Per-sample predictions for all models and labelings are provided in Supplementary Table S5.⟧", hl=False)

# ---------------- Discussion
T(f"⟪Coverage-normalised burden.⟫ ⟦Normalisation removed the dependence of the total burden on depth: its Spearman correlation with the median read depth fell from {nd.loc['raw'].rho_total_vs_median_depth:.2f} to {nd.loc['normalised'].rho_total_vs_median_depth:.2f} (Table 4). At gene level the median absolute correlation with depth decreased from {nd.loc['raw'].median_abs_rho_gene_vs_depth:.2f} to {nd.loc['normalised'].median_abs_rho_gene_vs_depth:.2f}, and the share of genes with |ρ| ≥ 0.5 from {100*nd.loc['raw']['share_genes_abs_rho_ge_0.5']:.0f}% to {100*nd.loc['normalised']['share_genes_abs_rho_ge_0.5']:.0f}%. Sample identity was not affected: the nearest neighbour of every run was still the other run of its sample (14/14). The logistic regression changed markedly. With global loci, the balanced accuracy of {nm.loc['GSM-LOGO, global loci, raw'].observed_BA:.3f} reported previously fell to {nm.loc['GSM-LOGO, global loci, normalised'].observed_BA:.3f} (ROC-AUC {nm.loc['GSM-LOGO, global loci, normalised'].observed_AUC:.3f}, exact p = {nm.loc['GSM-LOGO, global loci, normalised'].exact_p_BA:.3f}), so part of that value was carried by differences in burden scale between runs rather than by the composition of the burden. With training-only loci the normalised burden gave {nm.loc['GSM-LOGO, training-only loci, normalised'].observed_BA:.3f} under sample-level and {nm.loc['Person-LOGO, training-only loci, normalised'].observed_BA:.3f} under person-level folds (exact p = {nm.loc['GSM-LOGO, training-only loci, normalised'].exact_p_BA:.3f} and {nm.loc['Person-LOGO, training-only loci, normalised'].exact_p_BA:.3f}). No design was significant with either representation.⟧")
TCAP("Table 4 – Raw and coverage-normalised gene burden: dependence on depth, sample identification and grouped logistic regression")
rows = [["Quantity", "Raw burden", "Normalised burden"],
        ["Total burden vs median depth, Spearman ρ", f"{nd.loc['raw'].rho_total_vs_median_depth:.2f}", f"{nd.loc['normalised'].rho_total_vs_median_depth:.2f}"],
        ["Gene burden vs median depth, median |ρ|", f"{nd.loc['raw'].median_abs_rho_gene_vs_depth:.2f}", f"{nd.loc['normalised'].median_abs_rho_gene_vs_depth:.2f}"],
        ["Genes with |ρ| ≥ 0.5, %", f"{100*nd.loc['raw']['share_genes_abs_rho_ge_0.5']:.1f}", f"{100*nd.loc['normalised']['share_genes_abs_rho_ge_0.5']:.1f}"],
        ["1-NN sample identification", f"{int(round(14*nd.loc['raw'].nn_sample_identification))}/14", f"{int(round(14*nd.loc['normalised'].nn_sample_identification))}/14"]]
for lbl, key in [("GSM folds, global loci", "GSM-LOGO, global loci"), ("GSM folds, training-only loci", "GSM-LOGO, training-only loci"), ("Person folds, training-only loci", "Person-LOGO, training-only loci")]:
    rr, nn_ = nm.loc[f"{key}, raw"], nm.loc[f"{key}, normalised"]
    rows.append([f"Logistic regression, {lbl}: BA (exact p) / ROC-AUC", f"{rr.observed_BA:.3f} ({rr.exact_p_BA:.3f}) / {rr.observed_AUC:.3f}", f"{nn_.observed_BA:.3f} ({nn_.exact_p_BA:.3f}) / {nn_.observed_AUC:.3f}"])
b.data_table(rows, [8.6, 3.6, 3.6], font_size=18,
             note="Note: compiled by the authors from I_norm_coverage_dependence.tsv and I_norm_exact_summary.tsv (Supplementary Table S6). Normalised burden = burden / PASS calls of the run × 10⁵. Logistic regression as in the previous analysis (L2, balanced class weights); exact p over 35 labelings.")
T("【Discussion.】 ⟦The audit shows that a technically sound pipeline can still produce features that describe the wrong thing. Every module pointed in the same direction: call counts followed coverage, recurrent loci reproduced the identity of each sample at every reasonable threshold, two GEO samples turned out to be one person, and grouped validation found no condition signal. None of these properties is visible in a run-level classification score, which in the previous version of this manuscript reached 0.97; all of them become visible once the pipeline reports its intermediate outputs.⟧", hl=False)
T("⟦The threshold sweep is the most useful single diagnostic. Recurrence filters are usually justified as a way to remove private noise and keep shared biology, but in a cohort of a few individuals the loci shared by several runs are mostly the germline alleles of the individuals themselves. The sweep makes this explicit: the sample structure survives until almost no features are left, whereas the condition structure never appears. Reporting the curve, rather than a single threshold, lets readers see whether a claimed signal depends on the filter.⟧", hl=False)
T("⟦Normalisation by the number of calls is the simplest correction for the coverage dependence found by the audit, and it worked for what it targets: the run-level link between depth and burden disappeared. It did not, however, change what the features describe: runs were still identified by sample. The grouped results moved in both directions – the 0.708 obtained with raw burden and global loci disappeared, while a value of the same size appeared under person-level folds (exact p = 0.086) – and none was significant, which shows how much a single grouped estimate depends on the scale of the features. A pipeline should therefore report count features both raw and normalised; a result that holds in only one of them is more likely to reflect depth than the class.⟧")
T("⟦The cost profile matters for planning. Splice-aware read splitting and haplotype calling of droplet scRNA-seq BAM files take several hours per run even on a modern workstation, while the downstream feature construction and an exhaustive permutation analysis take minutes. Storing the PASS VCF files and the per-run audit tables is therefore far cheaper than recomputing them, and a complete grouped validation with an exact null adds no meaningful cost.⟧", hl=False)
TCAP("Table 5 – Checklist for expressed-variant features from scRNA-seq")
rows = [["Check", "How it was done here", "Outcome"],
        ["Labels match the primary source", "GEO sample titles", "Previous labels inverted; corrected"],
        ["Unit of independence defined", "Runs, samples and persons distinguished", "2 runs per sample"],
        ["Sample identity verified by genotype", "Concordance at depth ≥ 20", "Sc_Skin1 = Sc_W2 (one person)"],
        ["Validation grouped at the highest identity level", "Sample- and person-level folds", "Applied"],
        ["Feature selection inside training folds", "Training-only recurrent loci", "Applied"],
        ["Preprocessing inside training folds", "Pipeline per fold", "Applied"],
        ["Significance against a group-level null", "Exact test over 35 labelings", "No design significant"],
        ["Technical covariates checked", "Depth, detected genes, BAM size", "Strong coverage dependence"],
        ["Count features normalised by coverage", "Burden per 10⁵ PASS calls", "Depth link removed; identity kept"],
        ["Filter thresholds shown as a curve", "k = 1–14 sweep", "No condition effect at any k"],
        ["Predictions, hashes and versions released", "Manifests, per-fold tables", "Released"]]
b.data_table(rows, [5.4, 5.0, 4.8], font_size=18, note="Note: compiled by the authors on the basis of this study and the recommendations of Whalen et al. (2022) and Kapoor and Narayanan (2023).")
T("⟦Several limitations apply. The audit covers one cohort of seven samples from six persons, so it can show that a strong condition signal is absent but not exclude a weak one. Variant calls from droplet scRNA-seq have low depth and are conditional on expression; the pipeline does not separate germline, somatic, RNA-editing and artefactual calls, and no matched DNA is available. The identity of Sc_Skin1 and Sc_W2 is inferred from genotypes and metadata and has not been confirmed by the data submitters. The BAM file of one run was not archived, so its BAM size is missing from the coverage analysis, and its expression library contains fewer counts (1,409) than that of its partner run (6,021), although its variant calls are almost identical in number; this run is retained but flagged. Finally, the runtime refers to one workstation configuration.⟧", hl=False)
T("【Conclusion.】 ⟦A reproducible pipeline was described that converts scRNA-seq alignments into expressed-variant gene-burden features with GATK, recurrence selection and exon-level aggregation, and that reports its own quality, sensitivity and cost. In a public scRNA-seq cohort with two tissue classes, hard filters removed about one fifth of the raw calls, call counts followed coverage, the recurrence filter preserved the identity of each sample at every threshold up to k = 10 but produced no condition effect at any threshold, two samples were found to come from one individual, and grouped validation with training-only features gave a balanced accuracy of 0.500 for six classifiers. Variant calling dominates the computational cost, whereas the audit itself is inexpensive. Normalising the burden by the number of calls, the new element of the pipeline, removed its dependence on depth but not the identity of the samples, and the balanced accuracy of 0.708 obtained with raw burden and global loci did not survive it.⟧", hl=False)
T("⟦The study yields practical recommendations: reconcile labels with the primary source, check sample identity by genotype concordance, report recurrence filters as sensitivity curves, report count features both raw and normalised by the number of calls, build all data-dependent features inside training folds, group validation by individual, and compare results with an exact or permutation null rather than with 0.5. With these checks, the pipeline can be applied to larger cohorts in which genuine class effects can be tested.⟧", hl=False)
T("【Generative AI use.】 ⟦Claude (Anthropic; model Claude Opus 5.5) was used in November 2026 to assist in revising the manuscript: writing analysis and figure scripts on the authors' instructions, drafting the revised text, tables and structured abstracts in English, Kazakh and Russian from the result files, and language editing. All analyses were run, checked and interpreted by the authors, who reviewed and edited all content and take full responsibility for the final manuscript.⟧", hl=False)
b.keep(146)
T("【Data and code availability.】 Source sequencing data: PRJNA736095 / GSE176415. Article-specific code, archived results and Supplementary Materials: https://github.com/dauletulyaidyn/pad-mutation-gene-burden-classification. ⟦Revision scripts light_00–light_05, result tables with SHA-256 input hashes and per-sample predictions are included in the Supplementary Materials.⟧", hl=False)
T("【Supplementary Materials.】 ⟦Supplementary Materials (NANRK_rev6_Supplementary_Materials.docx) contain Note S1 (label reconciliation), Note S2 (software, parameters and hashes) and Tables S1–S6: per-run call counts and filter flags (S1), coverage correlations (S2), the complete threshold sweep (S3), the GATK runtime per run and step (S4), the exact-null summary with per-sample predictions (S5) and the raw versus coverage-normalised burden (S6). The accompanying CSV files contain the full tables.⟧", hl=False)

